# Trellis.Microservices Sample — REST client requests
#
# Works with:
#   - VS Code: "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client)
#   - Visual Studio 2022 17.8+: built-in .http file support
#   - JetBrains Rider / IntelliJ: built-in HTTP client
#
# Prereq: Aspire AppHost running.
#   dotnet run --project examples/Sample/Sample.AppHost
#
# This boots Gateway (port 5001) + Orders + Billing and opens the Aspire dashboard
# (https://localhost:17051 by default). Logs, traces, and metrics from all three
# services flow into the dashboard so you can SEE the gateway -> orders / billing
# trace span fan-out in real time.
#
# See examples/Sample/README.md for the full walkthrough.

@host = http://localhost:5001

### 1. Orders — happy path (list)
# Sends an X-Test-Actor JSON envelope to the Gateway. The Gateway's
# DevelopmentActorProvider parses it, AddTrellisActorForwarding mints a fresh JWT
# (YARP matched route "orders-route", whose ClusterId is "orders";
# AudiencePerCluster uses that ClusterId, so the JWT audience is "orders")
# and YARP forwards to the Orders service. Orders validates via OIDC
# discovery (fetches JWKS from Gateway), hydrates the Actor via
# TrellisInternalJwtActorProvider, and runs ListOrdersQuery (static-
# permission only — any actor with orders:read can list every order).
# Open the Aspire dashboard's Traces tab to see the Gateway -> Orders span.
#
# Expected: 200 OK + an array of seeded OrderResponse DTOs (order-1 + order-2).
GET {{host}}/api/orders
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"alice","Permissions":["orders:read","orders:write"],"ForbiddenPermissions":["orders:delete"],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"acme-corp"}}

### 2. Billing — happy path (DIFFERENT actor envelope, DIFFERENT route)
# Different X-Test-Actor (this one carries billing:read instead of orders:delete
# in ForbiddenPermissions). Different URL prefix. YARP matches route
# "billing-route" (ClusterId="billing"), AddTrellisActorForwarding mints a
# SEPARATE JWT with audience="billing", forwards to the Billing service.
# Billing's JwtBearer pins audience="billing" so it accepts; the token minted
# for /api/orders would NOT validate here (per-cluster audience pinning — see
# ARCHITECTURE.md §7).
#
# Expected: 200 OK + "service":"billing" + Actor echoing the envelope below.
GET {{host}}/api/billing
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"alice","Permissions":["orders:read","billing:read"],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"acme-corp"}}

### 3. Orders — different tenant (carol / contoso)
# Proves a non-empty tenant_id passes Orders' RequiredAttributes gate (without
# tenant_id the request would 401 — see scenario 4). Orders does NOT echo
# Actor.Attributes in its response (it returns OrderResponse[]); to inspect
# the hydrated tenant_id on the downstream side, send the same X-Test-Actor
# to /api/billing (Billing's endpoint echoes the full Actor).
GET {{host}}/api/orders
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"carol","Permissions":["orders:read"],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"contoso"}}

### 4. Missing tenant_id — fail-closed via RequiredAttributes
# Expected: 401 Unauthorized. JwtBearer accepts the token (signature / iss /
# aud / lifetime all OK). The downstream Orders service's
# TrellisInternalJwtActorProvider has RequiredAttributes = ["tenant_id"] so
# the actor provider returns Maybe.None when the tenant claim is absent,
# which short-circuits the business handler. This is an actor-provider
# contract failure (NOT a JwtBearer invalid_token failure); the framework's
# defense against a downstream ever evaluating a request without knowing
# which tenant it belongs to.
GET {{host}}/api/orders
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"bob","Permissions":["orders:read"],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{}}

### 5. No actor header at all
# Expected: 401 Unauthorized. DevelopmentActorProvider falls back to the default
# actor ("guest" with no permissions/attributes); without tenant_id the downstream
# fails closed.
GET {{host}}/api/orders

### 6. Unknown route — YARP no-match
# Expected: 404 Not Found. Proves YARP is actually doing path-based routing —
# only /api/orders/{**catch-all} and /api/billing/{**catch-all} are mapped.
GET {{host}}/api/unknown
X-Test-Actor: {"Id":"alice","Permissions":[],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"acme"}}

### 7. OIDC discovery document (published by Gateway via MapTrellisDiscoveryEndpoint)
# This is what the downstream services' AddJwtBearer(o.Authority = "http://localhost:5001")
# auto-fetches to learn where the JWKS lives.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/openid-configuration

### 8. JWKS document (the Gateway's public signing keys)
# Downstream services fetch discovery + JWKS LAZILY on first JWT validation,
# cache the metadata + signing keys, and refresh on metadata-cache expiry or
# when an inbound JWT carries an unknown kid. The "kid" JWT HEADER PARAMETER
# (not a claim) selects which JWKS key to verify against.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/jwks.json

###
# ===================================================================
# Resource-based authorization — John & Jill ownership demo
# ===================================================================
#
# Two actors share the SAME permissions (orders:read + orders:write); the
# authorization split happens at the RESOURCE layer, not the claim layer.
# Each actor OWNS one order (seeded at Orders process startup):
#   - order-1 → ownerId="john", customer="Contoso", total=99
#   - order-2 → ownerId="jill", customer="Globex Inc", total=149
#
# Policy: view-any (read), edit-mine (write — owner-only).
#
# Outcome matrix (see ARCHITECTURE.md §11):
#   - GET  /api/orders/{id} → 200 for either actor regardless of ownership
#   - PUT  /api/orders/{id} → 204 if actor.Id == order.OwnerId, else 403
#
# All resource auth runs INSIDE the Orders microservice. The Gateway is
# unaware of resource ownership; it just mints the JWT and forwards.
#
# Each load through InMemoryOrderRepository.FindByIdAsync increments the
# `orders.resource_loads` counter (visible in Aspire dashboard Metrics tab).
# The single-resource handlers (GetOrderHandler / UpdateOrderHandler) inject
# IAuthorizedResource<,> instead of IOrderRepository, so the counter ticks
# exactly ONCE per GET/PUT-by-id request — proving the v4 typed-accessor
# pattern eliminates the duplicate load. (ListOrdersHandler intentionally
# uses IOrderRepository.ListAllAsync because list isn't a single-resource
# auth path — no IIdentifyResource<,>.)

@john = {"Id":"john","Permissions":["orders:read","orders:write"],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"acme-corp"}}
@jill = {"Id":"jill","Permissions":["orders:read","orders:write"],"ForbiddenPermissions":[],"Attributes":{"tenant_id":"acme-corp"}}

### 9. List all orders (any actor with orders:read)
GET {{host}}/api/orders
X-Test-Actor: {{john}}

### 10. John GETs order-1 (his) → 200 (view-any)
GET {{host}}/api/orders/order-1
X-Test-Actor: {{john}}

### 11. John GETs order-2 (jill's) → 200 (view-any allows non-owner read)
GET {{host}}/api/orders/order-2
X-Test-Actor: {{john}}

### 12. Jill GETs order-1 (john's) → 200 (view-any)
GET {{host}}/api/orders/order-1
X-Test-Actor: {{jill}}

### 13. Jill GETs order-2 (hers) → 200
GET {{host}}/api/orders/order-2
X-Test-Actor: {{jill}}

### 14. John PUTs order-1 (his) → 204 No Content (write-mine: owner can edit)
PUT {{host}}/api/orders/order-1
X-Test-Actor: {{john}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "Customer": "Contoso (updated by john)",
  "Total": 109.99
}

### 15. John PUTs order-2 (jill's) → 403 Forbidden (write-mine: non-owner blocked)
PUT {{host}}/api/orders/order-2
X-Test-Actor: {{john}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "Customer": "Globex (john tried to edit)",
  "Total": 0.01
}

### 16. Jill PUTs order-2 (hers) → 204
PUT {{host}}/api/orders/order-2
X-Test-Actor: {{jill}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "Customer": "Globex Inc (updated by jill)",
  "Total": 159.99
}

### 17. Jill PUTs order-1 (john's) → 403 (non-owner blocked)
PUT {{host}}/api/orders/order-1
X-Test-Actor: {{jill}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "Customer": "Contoso (jill tried to edit)",
  "Total": 0.01
}

###
# ===================================================================
# Load-once proof — orders.resource_loads counter
# ===================================================================
# Send #10–#13 six times via the "Send Request" button. After 6 sends of #10
# the Aspire dashboard's Metrics tab should show
# `orders.resource_loads{order.id="order-1"}` increased by exactly 6 (not 12).
# That difference is the v4 typed-accessor — handlers read the SAME order
# instance the ResourceAuthorizationBehavior loaded, without re-querying the
# repository. See ARCHITECTURE.md §11.5.
